---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="Morning Briefing - 08/13/26: Power Grab TX"
  description="Keller's council passed a resolution against neighboring Westlake's approved data center and voted to hire outside counsel, escalating a DFW border fight over a project near homes."
>
  <div class="post-page">
    <div class="post-content">
      <h1>⭐ Morning Briefing — Texas &amp; DFW AI Data Centers, Grid, Land Use</h1>
      <p class="briefing-subtitle">(Latest updates through August 13, 2026)</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <h2>🔌 1. ERCOT &amp; Grid Signals</h2>
      <p class="section-tagline">The 474 GW Freeze Becomes a White House Talking Point</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Natural Gas Intelligence reported Aug. 12 that Texas's pause on new data-center grid connections now affects roughly 474 GW of pending interconnection requests statewide — more than five times ERCOT's all-time peak demand — as the audit Gov. Abbott ordered Aug. 3 continues.</li>
        <li>The dispute over that pause kept escalating this week: President Trump called Abbott's moratorium "a mistake" in comments that aired Aug. 10-11, and Abbott has been publicly defending the pause since, with Fox 4 Dallas-Fort Worth and CBS Austin both carrying dueling statements from the two.</li>
        <li>ERCOT's Aug. 3 market notice (M-A080326-01) confirmed it suspended the Batch Zero Large Load classification notices originally due Aug. 7 and said it will report back to the Public Utility Commission's Aug. 20 open meeting.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> A paperwork pause that started Aug. 3 has become the biggest flashpoint in Texas energy politics, with the White House now publicly needling the governor over it.</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <h2>🏗️ 2. Data Center Construction &amp; Corporate Activity</h2>
      <p class="section-tagline">Amazon's Pecos County Build Becomes the Compliance Poster Child</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Amazon has bought roughly 8,400 acres in Pecos County and is actively clearing land for a data-center campus near Fort Stockton; three separate construction permits for the buildings were filed in Texas in early August, per Yahoo Finance and the San Antonio Current.</li>
        <li>USA Today and Houston Public Media reported Aug. 10-11 that eight major tech companies — including Meta, OpenAI, Google and Amazon — have now agreed to Gov. Abbott's new data-center energy and water standards; Meta said in its statement it already pays the full cost of its facilities' energy and water use.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> Amazon's Pecos County build is now the clearest example of the land rush and the corporate-compliance push happening at the same time, in the same county.</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <h2>🌎 3. Environmental &amp; Air-Permit Activity (TCEQ)</h2>
      <p class="section-tagline">"Standard Permits" Skip the Public Comment Larger Air Permits Require</p>
      <ul>
        <li>TCEQ's pending-permits list shows a new-source-review air permit application from Westline TX Holdings for a Beacon Data Centers project near San Angelo (Tom Green County); a local official publicly pressed for clarity on the filing this week, per ConchoValleyHomepage.</li>
        <li>A KSAT investigation published Aug. 11 detailed how data-center operators are increasingly seeking TCEQ "standard permits" for their gas-fired backup and primary power plants — a permit category that skips the public-comment step required for larger air permits.</li>
        <li>Today's search turned up no new permit filings specifically in the eight DFW-area counties this briefing tracks (Collin, Ellis, Johnson, Denton, Wise, Tarrant, Parker, Hood); the dated TCEQ activity found today centers on West Texas, near San Angelo.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> TCEQ's own permit categories are becoming part of the story — "standard permits" let gas plants serving data centers skip the public input that larger air permits require.</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <h2>🏛️ 4. Land-Use &amp; Zoning Developments</h2>
      <p class="section-tagline">Fort Worth's Moratorium Advances While Keller and Westlake Head Toward Court</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Fort Worth City Council voted unanimously Aug. 11, formalized Aug. 12, to begin the process for a 90-day moratorium on new data-center construction; the earliest the pause could take effect is Feb. 16, 2027, per Community Impact.</li>
        <li>Fort Worth also stood up an 11-member Data Center &amp; Infrastructure Commission to evaluate future developments, with its first report due at a Nov. 3 work session, per KERA News.</li>
        <li>Just north of Fort Worth, Keller's city council called an emergency meeting Aug. 12 that ran past midnight into today. Residents packed the room to oppose a roughly 88-acre data center that Westlake's Planning and Zoning Commission had already approved near the Marshall Ridge neighborhood. The council voted unanimously to retain outside legal counsel and sent Westlake a resolution asking it to pause the project, with Mayor Ross McMullin telling the crowd he'll pursue "every legal option available."</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> The Keller-Westlake dispute shows the zoning fight moving past single-city moratoriums into neighboring cities threatening legal action over projects just across their shared border.</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <h2>⚡ 5. Power-First Strategies &amp; On-Site Generation</h2>
      <p class="section-tagline">A 7.65 GW Gas Plant Puts Amazon at the Center of the Emissions Debate</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The New York Times and Ars Technica reported this week that Amazon is backing a large natural-gas plant — reported at up to 7.65 GW across 35 turbines — to power its Pecos County AI campus, with critics warning it could become one of the largest single sources of power-plant pollution in the country.</li>
        <li>Forbes cited a Cleanview analysis (July 2026) finding roughly 90 GW of behind-the-meter gas-generation capacity for U.S. data centers now in some stage of development, with xAI currently accounting for most of the roughly 2 GW already running.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> Amazon's Pecos County gas plant is shaping up as the test case for how far "power-first," build-your-own-generation strategies can go before drawing national environmental scrutiny.</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <h2>📍 Today's Watch List</h2>
      <p class="section-tagline">Actionable Signals for Thursday, August 13</p>

      <h3>ERCOT</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>PUCT's Aug. 20 open meeting, where ERCOT is due to report back on the Batch Zero pause</li>
        <li>Whether the Trump-Abbott public dispute over the moratorium produces any policy change</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>PUC</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Any formal order following Gov. Abbott's Aug. 3 audit directive</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>TCEQ</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Movement on the Westline TX Holdings / Beacon Data Centers air permit near San Angelo</li>
        <li>Any new DFW-county (Collin, Ellis, Johnson, Denton, Wise, Tarrant, Parker, Hood) air-permit filings</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>County Zoning</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Westlake's response to Keller's resolution and threatened legal action</li>
        <li>Fort Worth's next procedural steps toward finalizing its 90-day moratorium</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Corporate Filings</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Additional construction permits tied to Amazon's Pecos County campus</li>
        <li>Any further companies joining the eight that have agreed to Abbott's data-center standards</li>
      </ul>

